<div class="prose max-w-prose">

Seall ECDIS already had an existing website, but as the industry evolved and new
S-100 navigation standards emerged, the business needed more than a basic
product page. They needed a website that could explain complex technology,
position their offering clearly, and educate the market around the shift in the
marine navigation landscape.

## The challenge

The product offering was expanding beyond existing capabilities, and the brand
needed to speak to both technical buyers and a wider audience who needed to
understand what was changing. The website had to do more than market features
— it had to help people understand the standards, the relevance of the
technology, and why it mattered.

## The solution

I built a new website in Webflow designed to support both education and
conversion. The site was structured to introduce the company, explain the
complexity of the sector, and clearly communicate how Seall ECDIS supports the
transition to modern standards and S-100-driven tools.

The design focuses on clarity and credibility: a strong information hierarchy,
clear product messaging, and a more confident presentation of the company’s
expertise. Instead of relying on simple product marketing alone, the site helps
position Seall ECDIS as a knowledgeable, authoritative voice in a technical
space.

## Beyond the website

Following the website work, I also took over a large share of Seall ECDIS's
LinkedIn marketing. The channel was transformed from sparse, low-impact reposts
into a more consistent stream of branded content that demonstrates sector
knowledge, explains technical developments, and builds trust with the market.

This included a shift from passive updates to strategic, value-led content that
showcases expertise and keeps the brand visible in a specialist industry.

## The outcome

The result was a more informed, more confident digital presence that supports
both product education and brand authority. The website became a stronger tool
for explaining the offering, while the LinkedIn activity helped extend that
message in a more regular and engaging way.

